    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok so it's like checking the signal against different rhythms to see what sticks. but if a signal is really noisy or chaotic, can the fourier transform still pick out the actual signal, or does the noise just drown everything out?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When noise is present, it introduces energy across many frequencies, effectively smearing the results of the transform. However, if the underlying signal is strong and periodic, it will have distinct, concentrated peaks in the frequency domain while the noise will appear as a broader, lower-intensity background energy. Techniques like filtering or windowing are then used to enhance the signal peaks and suppress the noise floor.",
      "source": "target",
